; research/structural/bounded.bl — candidate-RESTRICTED authorized top-k over a
; datom lexical posting index, with an explicit work-budget contract, a
; provenance-bearing cursor, and a genuinely PAGED ordered-posting read for the
; single-term case.
;
; ── The proposition (PLAN-165 audit, opportunity 6) ──────────────────────
;
; Knowledger's lexical fallback (`search-one`) ranks the corpus, over-fetches a
; FIXED `6*k` window and only THEN applies rights. Two costs fall out of that
; shape:
;
;   1. WORK — the lexical scan reads :chunk/text across the whole corpus, so a
;      query costs O(corpus) even when its terms are selective.
;   2. CORRECTNESS — a fixed global overfetch followed by a post-filter can
;      return FEWER than k permitted rows while permitted rows sit just below
;      the window. The audit calls this out explicitly: "search-one:526-530
;      overfetches 6*k then applies rights, so it can return fewer than k
;      despite allowed rows lower in ranking."
;
; ── What this spike actually establishes (READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING IT) ───
;
; The candidate path is POSTING-RESTRICTED, not page-bounded. Precisely:
;
;   • `candidate-scores` reads EVERY posting row for every query term
;     (sum_t df(t) rows) and materializes a score for EVERY matching document.
;   • `ranked-score-pairs` SORTS that whole scored candidate set.
;   • only the final authorization walk (`walk-authorized` / `walk-page`)
;     stops at k permitted rows.
;
; So a first "page" is NOT O(page): the posting read and the ranking are
; O(Σ df(terms)) and O(c log c). What the page bounds is the ACL entity reads
; after ranking, and `page-topk-db` never scans `:doc/text` — that is the whole
; of the old claim. An earlier revision of this file claimed the page "never
; walks the whole candidate set"; that was WRONG and is corrected here.
;
; A genuinely page-bounded top-k needs a posting index ORDERED BY RANK plus a
; paged read. This file provides exactly that for a SINGLE TERM:
;
;   • `:post/rank` is a second indexed string attribute whose value encodes
;     (term, score DESC, doc-eid ASC) in lexicographic order, so an AVET prefix
;     read on `:post/rank` yields a term's postings already in rank order.
;   • `posting-page` reads that prefix in bounded pages through the store's
;     `PageScan` capability (`datom.store/page-datoms`).
;   • `ordered-topk-page` walks those pages, applying the authorization gate
;     DURING the walk, and stops once k permitted rows are found, so its first
;     page reads O(page + denied) posting rows — on a substrate that actually
;     implements `PageScan` (fjall / durable). On the in-memory ETS substrate
;     `page-datoms` documents a materialize-the-range fallback, so the same call
;     is honest-but-unbounded there; the return carries `:bounded?` so no caller
;     can read the ETS path as bounded.
;
; General MULTIKEY scoring (several query terms summed) has no ordered index
; here. It is a POSTING-WIDE fallback: under a budget it either completes
; exactly or REFUSES/incompletes — it never presents a partial ranking as the
; answer. See `budgeted-topk-db`.
;
; ── The work-budget / honesty contract ──────────────────────────────────
;
; Every verdict carries `:status`:
;
;   :exact       the returned `:hits` ARE the true top-k. `:total` present.
;   :incomplete  the budget stopped the work before the rank order was known.
;                `:hits` is [] on purpose — a partial ranking is NOT an answer.
;                A `:resume` cursor and the real counters are returned instead.
;   :refused     the work was declined up front (budget cannot cover the known
;                posting rows, or a cursor did not match this db/query/principal).
;                `:hits` is [].
;
; "unknown is never proven": no code path here returns `:exact` unless every
; candidate was ranked and every permitted row up to k was seen.
;
; ── Cursor provenance ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; A cursor is NOT `[eid score]`. It carries the store identity, the basis, the
; query/term, the principal, and the order identity. `page-topk-db` and
; `ordered-topk-page` REJECT a cursor whose provenance does not match the db
; value they are given, instead of trusting the caller to have pinned it. A
; caller that holds one `datom/db` VALUE pages freely against it (basis stable);
; a caller that re-derives the db after a write must start over, which is the
; fail-closed choice: an old-basis cursor replayed on a new basis can resurrect
; a revoked row or skip one.
;
; ── What is verified, and by what ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; `bounded_test.bl` uses the existing logic module `veritas`:
;   • finite EXHAUSTION: the authorized ranked walk equals the full
;     authorized-sort oracle over a declared finite scenario domain;
;   • finite EXHAUSTION: a budgeted walk is either exact-and-equal-to-oracle, or
;     non-exact with NO hits — never a partial ranking presented as the answer;
;   • finite EXHAUSTION (negative control): the naive fixed-overfetch design
;     disagrees with the oracle, and veritas returns the witness;
;   • a z3 SYMBOLIC obligation for the rank comparator's asymmetry.
; The datom-backed path is checked against the datom-backed oracle, and the
; store boundary is counted (`datom.profile`), with metrics that separate
; POSTING rows read, RANKED candidates, and EXAMINED candidates. The datom path
; is tested, not proved.
;
; ── Production seam (see bounded.md) ────────────────────────────────────
;
; The ordered posting attribute (`:post/rank`) is the schema seam. It is a
; DERIVED projection of `:post/term` + `:post/tf` + `:post/doc`; maintain it
; with the existing derived/projection machinery, not a parallel cache. The
; genuinely-bounded single-term page needs a substrate that implements
; `datom.store/PageScan`; the general multikey path stays a posting-wide
; fallback behind the budget contract until an ordered multi-term index exists.

(ns research.structural.bounded
  (:require [datom]
            [datom.db]
            [datom.index]
            [datom.store]))

; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; 1. THE PURE KERNEL — the ranking/authorization decision as data
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; A "scenario" is a pure description of a candidate set:
;   {:docs [{:eid int :score int :readable bool :live bool} …]
;    :k    int
;    :overfetch int}   ; only the naive control reads it
;
; Splitting the decision from the store is what lets veritas exhaust a finite
; domain: the same comparator, the same gate, the same walk the datom path
; uses, over literal inputs. The datom path below is the same computation
; with the store supplying candidates and metadata.

(defn- rank-key
  "The total order's sort key: score DESCENDING, then eid ASCENDING. The eid
   component is the identity tie-break — two docs with equal score never swap
   between calls or between pages."
  [pair]
  [(- 0 (get pair 1)) (get pair 0)])

(defn- doc-rank-key
  "`rank-key` over a scenario doc record."
  [d]
  [(- 0 (get d :score)) (get d :eid)])

(defn- permitted?
  "The authorization predicate in one place: LIVE and READABLE. Sparse ACLs are
   simply `:readable false` on most rows; a tombstone is `:live false`."
  [d]
  (and (get d :live) (get d :readable)))

(defn oracle-topk-pure
  "THE ORACLE: consider every document, keep the permitted ones, order by
   (score desc, eid asc), take k. The exhaustive-sort reference the bounded
   path must reproduce exactly."
  [s]
  (let [docs (get s :docs)
        k (get s :k)
        permitted (filter permitted? docs)
        ranked (sort-by doc-rank-key permitted)]
    (vec (take k (map (fn [d] (get d :eid)) ranked)))))

(defn ranked-walk-pure
  "THE SOUND DESIGN: rank EVERY candidate (permitted or not), then WALK in
   ranked order, keeping the first k that are permitted. It continues PAST a
   denied candidate, so a permitted document below it is never missed. This is
   the kernel the datom path realizes."
  [s]
  (let [docs (get s :docs)
        k (get s :k)
        ranked (sort-by doc-rank-key docs)]
    (loop [rs ranked kept []]
      (if (or (= k (count kept)) (empty? rs))
        kept
        (let [d (first rs)]
          (recur (rest rs)
                 (if (permitted? d) (conj kept (get d :eid)) kept)))))))

(defn ordered-walk-pure
  "THE PAGE-BOUNDED DESIGN, as a kernel: walk `ordered` — document records
   ALREADY in rank order, which is what the ordered posting index supplies —
   and keep the first k permitted, reporting how many were examined and whether
   the list was exhausted. It TRUSTS the supplied order, so the parity law over
   it is NOT a tautology: feed it an out-of-order list and it diverges from the
   oracle."
  [ordered k]
  (loop [rs ordered kept [] seen 0]
    (if (or (= k (count kept)) (empty? rs))
      {:hits (vec (map (fn [d] (get d :eid)) kept))
       :examined seen
       :more? (not (empty? rs))}
      (let [d (first rs)]
        (recur (rest rs)
               (if (permitted? d) (conj kept d) kept)
               (+ seen 1))))))

(defn ordered-page-parity?
  "The law linking the page-bounded walk to the oracle: WHEN the supplied order
   is the rank order, the k-row page equals the oracle. Two independently
   written functions are compared, and a mis-ordered input refutes the law, so
   this is a real property rather than a restatement."
  [s]
  (= (oracle-topk-pure s)
     (get (ordered-walk-pure (sort-by doc-rank-key (get s :docs)) (get s :k)) :hits)))

(defn fixed-overfetch-pure
  "THE NAIVE WRONG DESIGN, kept as the negative control: rank every candidate
   globally (NO authorization), take the top `k * overfetch`, and only THEN
   filter permitted and take k. A permitted row ranked below the window is
   silently dropped — the existing `search-one` bug, isolated."
  [s]
  (let [docs (get s :docs)
        k (get s :k)
        of (get s :overfetch 6)
        ranked (sort-by doc-rank-key docs)
        window (take (* k of) ranked)
        permitted (filter permitted? window)]
    (vec (take k (map (fn [d] (get d :eid)) permitted)))))

(defn parity-holds?
  "The positive law, as a predicate the logic module can quantify over: the
   authorized ranked walk agrees with the full authorized-sort oracle."
  [s]
  (= (oracle-topk-pure s) (ranked-walk-pure s)))

(defn overfetch-misses?
  "The negative law: the naive fixed-overfetch control DISAGREES with the
   oracle on `s`. `veritas/exists` over a finite domain returns the first such
   scenario as a witness."
  [s]
  (not= (oracle-topk-pure s) (fixed-overfetch-pure s)))

(defn walk-with-budget-pure
  "THE BUDGET KERNEL: walk the ranked candidates applying the gate, but stop
   once `:exam-budget` candidates have been examined. If k permitted were found
   (or the candidates ran out) the verdict is `:exact` and the hits ARE the
   oracle's; if the budget stopped it first, the verdict is `:incomplete` and
   the hits are [] — the invariant that a partial ranking is never an answer."
  [s]
  (let [ranked (sort-by doc-rank-key (get s :docs))
        k (get s :k)
        budget (get s :exam-budget)]
    (loop [rs ranked kept [] seen 0]
      (cond
        (= k (count kept)) {:status :exact :hits (vec (map (fn [d] (get d :eid)) kept)) :examined seen}
        (empty? rs) {:status :exact :hits (vec (map (fn [d] (get d :eid)) kept)) :examined seen}
        (and (not (nil? budget)) (>= seen budget)) {:status :incomplete :hits [] :examined seen}
        :else (let [d (first rs)]
                (recur (rest rs)
                       (if (permitted? d) (conj kept d) kept)
                       (+ seen 1)))))))

(defn budget-walk-is-exact-or-empty?
  "THE HONESTY LAW: a budgeted walk is EXACT only when it equals the oracle, and
   non-exact verdicts carry NO hits. A bug that leaked a partial ranking as
   `:exact`, or attached hits to an `:incomplete`, refutes this."
  [s]
  (let [r (walk-with-budget-pure s)]
    (if (= :exact (get r :status))
      (= (get r :hits) (oracle-topk-pure s))
      (= [] (get r :hits)))))

; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; 2. THE DATOM-BACKED CANDIDATE PATH
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; Schema this spike expects (the production index schema seam):
;
;   :doc/text    string        ; the corpus row's text (any value column)
;   :doc/live    boolean       ; soft tombstone (hard retraction also works)
;   :doc/public  boolean       ; readable by every principal
;   :doc/reader  ref  many     ; the sparse ACL: principals who may read it
;   :post/term   string, INDEX ; the lexical posting key — AVET prefix read
;   :post/doc    ref           ; posting -> document (AVET + VAET maintained)
;   :post/tf     long          ; the posting's rank contribution
;   :post/rank   string, INDEX ; DERIVED rank-ordered key (ordered page only)
;
; A posting is a separate entity so that `:post/term` can be indexed: that
; index is the candidate-restriction primitive. `:post/rank` is a second,
; derived index; it is what makes a SINGLE-TERM page genuinely bounded.

(defn postings-for-term
  "The posting entities whose term is exactly `term`, read through the AVET
   index on `:post/term`. One bounded store read of `df(term)` rows — NOT a
   corpus scan, but NOT a page: the whole posting set for the term is read.
   This is the candidate-restriction primitive."
  [db term]
  (map datom/datom-e (datom/datoms db :avet :post/term term)))

(defn all-doc-eids
  "Every document entity, by the full AEVT column read of `:doc/text`. This is
   the ORACLE's read — deliberately corpus-wide, the cost the candidate path
   avoids."
  [db]
  (distinct (map datom/datom-e (datom/datoms db :aevt :doc/text))))

(defn all-posting-eids
  "Every posting entity, by the full AEVT column read of `:post/term`. The read
   the rank-index projection walks (a derived build, not a query path)."
  [db]
  (distinct (map datom/datom-e (datom/datoms db :aevt :post/term))))

(defn doc-score-db
  "`doc`'s summed `:post/tf` over the postings whose term is in `terms`, by
   reading the document's postings through the AVET index on `:post/doc`. A
   corpus-wide oracle read: every doc pays its own posting count."
  [db doc terms]
  (let [tset (set terms)]
    (reduce
      (fn [acc p]
        (let [e (datom/entity db p)]
          (if (contains? tset (:post/term e))
            (+ acc (or (:post/tf e) 0))
            acc)))
      0
      (map datom/datom-e (datom/datoms db :avet :post/doc doc)))))

(defn authorized?
  "The store-side authorization gate: LIVE and (PUBLIC or READER contains
   `principal`). Read through `datom/entity`, one bounded EAVT read of the
   document's own datoms. Sparse ACLs are a vector that simply lacks the
   principal; revocation is the absence of a `:doc/reader` datom. Fail-closed:
   an unknown principal is not public and not in the reader set."
  [db principal doc]
  (let [e (datom/entity db doc)]
    (and (= true (:doc/live e))
         (or (= true (:doc/public e))
             (if (some (fn [r] (= principal r)) (:doc/reader e)) true false)))))

(defn- candidate-scores-counted
  "`candidate-scores` WITH a real counter: returns
   `{:scores {doc-eid → summed tf} :posting-rows n}` where `n` is the number of
   posting rows actually materialized (Σ df over the query terms). The counter
   is the store read, not a relabelling of the sort."
  [db terms]
  (reduce
    (fn [acc term]
      (reduce
        (fn [acc2 p]
          (let [e (datom/entity db p)
                d (:post/doc e)
                tf (or (:post/tf e) 0)
                scores (get acc2 :scores)]
            {:scores (assoc scores d (+ (or (get scores d) 0) tf))
             :posting-rows (+ (get acc2 :posting-rows) 1)}))
        acc
        (postings-for-term db term)))
    {:scores {} :posting-rows 0}
    terms))

(defn candidate-scores
  "`{doc-eid → summed tf}` over the postings for `terms` ONLY. Reads
   `sum_t df(t)` posting rows plus one entity read per posting; never touches a
   document outside the posting set and never scans `:doc/text`. NB: this
   materializes the WHOLE candidate set — it is posting-restricted, not
   page-bounded."
  [db terms]
  (get (candidate-scores-counted db terms) :scores))

(defn ranked-score-pairs
  "A candidate score map as an ordered `[doc-eid score]` list, by
   (score desc, eid asc). `keys` order is unspecified, so the sort is what
   makes the result deterministic — the stable-rank requirement. NB: this sorts
   the WHOLE scored candidate set; there is no partial order to stop early on."
  [scores]
  (sort-by rank-key
           (mapv (fn [d] [d (get scores d)]) (keys scores))))

(defn- hits->maps
  "[doc score] pairs as result maps."
  [pairs]
  (vec (map (fn [p] {:eid (get p 0) :score (get p 1)}) pairs)))

(defn oracle-topk-db
  "THE FULL AUTHORIZED-SORT ORACLE over the store: enumerate EVERY document
   (AEVT on :doc/text), score each by its own postings, keep the permitted,
   order by (score desc, eid asc), take k. Corpus-wide by construction — the
   read the candidate path exists to avoid, and the reference it must equal."
  [db principal terms k]
  (let [docs (all-doc-eids db)
        tset (set terms)
        scored (filterv (fn [p] (> (get p 1) 0))
                        (mapv (fn [d] [d (doc-score-db db d tset)]) docs))
        permitted (filterv (fn [p] (authorized? db principal (get p 0))) scored)
        ranked (sort-by rank-key permitted)]
    {:status :exact
     :hits (hits->maps (take k ranked))
     :total (count permitted)
     :candidates (count scored)
     :posting-rows nil
     :ranked (count scored)
     :examined (count scored)}))

; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; 3. CURSOR PROVENANCE
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; A cursor records WHAT IT IS A POSITION IN: the store, the basis it was taken
; at, the query/term and principal it was ranked for, and the order identity.
; Resuming against a different db value, a different query, or a different
; principal is a REFUSAL, not a silent best-effort.

(defn store-identity
  "The store a db value reads, through any transparent wrapper. Used as the
   cursor's store component; `=` compares records structurally, and two dbs
   over the same connection share the one store."
  [db]
  (datom.store/identity-of (datom.db/store-of db)))

(def order-identity-score-desc-eid-asc :score-desc-eid-asc)
(def order-identity-post-rank :post-rank-score-desc-eid-asc)

(defn ranked-cursor
  "A provenance cursor for `page-topk-db`: position `pos` (`[eid score]`,
   exclusive) in the (score desc, eid asc) order, for a given store/basis/query/
   principal."
  [db principal terms pos]
  {:cursor/kind :ranked-topk
   :cursor/store (store-identity db)
   :cursor/basis (datom/basis-t db)
   :cursor/query (vec terms)
   :cursor/principal principal
   :cursor/order order-identity-score-desc-eid-asc
   :cursor/after pos})

(defn ranked-cursor-ok?
  "Whether `c` is a valid `page-topk-db` cursor for THIS db value, query and
   principal. False for nil or any provenance mismatch."
  [db principal terms c]
  (and (map? c)
       (not (nil? (get c :cursor/kind)))
       (= (get c :cursor/kind) :ranked-topk)
       (= (:cursor/store c) (store-identity db))
       (= (:cursor/basis c) (datom/basis-t db))
       (= (:cursor/query c) (vec terms))
       (= (:cursor/principal c) principal)
       (= (:cursor/order c) order-identity-score-desc-eid-asc)))

(defn- walk-authorized
  "Walk ranked `[doc score]` pairs applying the gate per candidate; collect the
   first `k` permitted AND count every permitted row seen (the total). A denied
   candidate advances the walk without consuming a hit. Examines EVERY candidate
   (the total needs it); this is the authoritative exact path."
  [db principal ranked k]
  (loop [rs ranked kept [] total 0 examined 0]
    (if (empty? rs)
      {:hits kept :total total :examined examined}
      (let [p (first rs)]
        (if (authorized? db principal (get p 0))
          (recur (rest rs)
                 (if (< (count kept) k) (conj kept p) kept)
                 (+ total 1)
                 (+ examined 1))
          (recur (rest rs) kept total (+ examined 1)))))))

(defn indexed-topk-db
  "The EXACT authorized top-k over the posting index.

   Returns `{:status :exact :hits [{:eid :score} …] :total n :candidates c
   :posting-rows r :ranked m :examined e}`:
     :hits          the first `k` PERMITTED candidates in (score desc, eid asc)
     :total         how many candidates are permitted (NOT the page length)
     :candidates    how many documents matched a query term at all
     :posting-rows  posting rows materialized by `candidate-scores`
     :ranked        candidates that entered the sort (== :candidates)
     :examined      ranked candidates the authorization walk visited

   This path visits EVERY candidate to report an honest `:total`; it is exact
   and, by construction, posting-wide rather than page-bounded."
  [db principal terms k]
  (if (<= k 0)
    {:status :exact :hits [] :total nil :candidates 0 :posting-rows 0 :ranked 0
     :examined 0 :note :non-positive-k}
    (let [scored (candidate-scores-counted db terms)
          scores (get scored :scores)
          ranked (ranked-score-pairs scores)
          r (walk-authorized db principal ranked k)]
      {:status :exact
       :hits (hits->maps (get r :hits))
       :total (get r :total)
       :candidates (count scores)
       :posting-rows (get scored :posting-rows)
       :ranked (count scores)
       :examined (get r :examined)})))

; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; 4. BOUNDED PAGES — how far the page actually bounds work
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

(defn- rank-less?
  "Does rank `a` come strictly before rank `b` in the total order? `a` and `b`
   are `[eid score]` pairs; used to resume a page after its cursor."
  [a b]
  (let [sa (get a 1) sb (get b 1)]
    (or (> sa sb) (and (= sa sb) (< (get a 0) (get b 0))))))

(defn- walk-page
  "Walk ranked `[doc score]` pairs applying the gate, stopping the moment `k`
   permitted rows are collected. Reports the last pair EXAMINED as the resume
   position, so resumption is exact even when the last candidate was denied.
   `:more?` says whether candidates remained unexamined. NB: `ranked` was
   ALREADY fully materialized and sorted by the caller — this walk only bounds
   the authorization reads, not the ranking."
  [db principal ranked k]
  (loop [rs ranked kept [] scanned 0 last nil]
    (if (or (= k (count kept)) (empty? rs))
      {:hits kept :cursor last :scanned scanned :more? (not (empty? rs))}
      (let [p (first rs)]
        (recur (rest rs)
               (if (authorized? db principal (get p 0)) (conj kept p) kept)
               (+ scanned 1)
               p)))))

(defn page-topk-db
  "ONE page of the authorized ranked result after provenance cursor `after` (a
   `ranked-cursor` map, or nil for the first page). Returns
   `{:status :exact|:refused :hits … :cursor … :more? bool :scanned n
     :candidates c :ranked m :posting-rows r :examined e}`.

   BOUNDS: the authorization walk stops at k permitted rows, so the ACL entity
   reads follow `page`; the candidate POSTING read and the SORT are still
   O(Σ df(terms) + c log c). Do NOT read a first page as O(page).

   A cursor whose provenance (store, basis, query, principal, order) does not
   match this db value is REFUSED — `:reason :cursor-mismatch` — rather than
   resumed on an assumption. There is no `:total` here: the page did not walk
   the whole candidate set."
  [db principal terms after k]
  (if (and (not (nil? after)) (not (ranked-cursor-ok? db principal terms after)))
    {:status :refused :reason :cursor-mismatch :hits [] :cursor nil :more? false
     :scanned 0 :candidates 0 :ranked 0 :posting-rows 0 :examined 0}
    (if (<= k 0)
      {:status :exact :hits [] :cursor nil :more? false :scanned 0
       :candidates 0 :ranked 0 :posting-rows 0 :examined 0 :note :non-positive-k}
      (let [scored (candidate-scores-counted db terms)
            scores (get scored :scores)
            ranked (ranked-score-pairs scores)
            pos (get after :cursor/after)
            ordered (if (nil? pos)
                      ranked
                      (filterv (fn [p] (rank-less? pos p)) ranked))
            r (walk-page db principal ordered k)]
        {:status :exact
         :hits (hits->maps (get r :hits))
         :cursor (if (get r :more?)
                   (ranked-cursor db principal terms (get r :cursor))
                   nil)
         :more? (get r :more?)
         :scanned (get r :scanned)
         :examined (get r :scanned)
         :candidates (count scores)
         :ranked (count scores)
         :posting-rows (get scored :posting-rows)}))))

(defn fixed-overfetch-db
  "THE NAIVE CONTROL on the real index: rank candidates globally with NO
   authorization, take the top `k * overfetch`, THEN filter permitted. Exists so
   the test can exhibit the miss against a live datom store — and so the bug is
   a named, removable design rather than a shape someone re-introduces."
  [db principal terms k overfetch]
  (let [scores (candidate-scores db terms)
        ranked (ranked-score-pairs scores)
        window (take (* k overfetch) ranked)
        permitted (filterv (fn [p] (authorized? db principal (get p 0))) window)]
    {:hits (hits->maps (take k permitted))}))

; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; 5. THE WORK-BUDGET CONTRACT
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; The multikey path cannot page its posting read (there is no ordered multi-term
; index), so a budget is enforced by ADMISSION: count the posting rows the query
; needs (through the store's bounded key count when it has one) and refuse
; before reading if the budget cannot cover them. When the substrate cannot
; count, the read proceeds and the budget is checked after; either way the
; verdict is exact or it carries no hits.

(defn posting-df
  "The number of posting rows for `term`: `{:count n :capped bool}` from the
   store's bounded key count, or nil when the substrate cannot count without
   reading. `limit` bounds the count when the caller only cares about a ceiling."
  ([db term] (posting-df db term nil))
  ([db term limit]
   (let [store (datom.db/store-of db)]
     (if (datom.store/count-capable? store)
       (let [lo (datom.index/key-prefix :avet :post/term term)
             hi (datom.index/prefix-end lo)
             r (datom.store/count-range store lo hi (or limit 1073741824))]
         r)
       nil))))

(defn- known-posting-rows
  "The summed known df for `terms` as `{:rows n :capped bool}`, or nil when any
   term's df is unknown. `:capped` true means `:rows` is a LOWER BOUND (the key
   count stopped at `limit`). Walks `count-range` per term, so it is itself a
   bounded per-term key count."
  [db terms limit]
  (loop [ts terms acc 0 capped false]
    (if (empty? ts)
      {:rows acc :capped capped}
      (let [r (posting-df db (first ts) limit)]
        (if (nil? r)
          nil
          (recur (rest ts) (+ acc (get r :count)) (or capped (get r :capped))))))))

(defn- verdict-from-scores
  "The shared exact/incomplete verdict once the posting read has happened.
   `scored` is the `candidate-scores-counted` result; the budget is re-checked against
   the real posting-row counter, so a substrate that could not count ahead of
   the read still cannot present a partial ranking as exact."
  [db principal terms k budget scored]
  (if (and (not (nil? budget)) (> (get scored :posting-rows) budget))
    {:status :incomplete :reason :posting-budget-exhausted-after-read
     :hits [] :total nil :candidates (count (get scored :scores))
     :posting-rows (get scored :posting-rows) :ranked 0 :examined 0
     :budget budget :resume (ranked-cursor db principal terms nil)}
    (let [scores (get scored :scores)
          ranked (ranked-score-pairs scores)
          r (walk-authorized db principal ranked k)]
      {:status :exact
       :hits (hits->maps (get r :hits))
       :total (get r :total)
       :candidates (count scores)
       :posting-rows (get scored :posting-rows)
       :ranked (count scores)
       :examined (get r :examined)
       :budget budget})))

(defn budgeted-topk-db
  "The EXACT-or-REFUSED authorized top-k under a posting-row budget.

   `opts` may hold `:max-posting-rows n`. Verdicts:
     :exact      `:hits`/`:total` are the true answer (budget not exceeded);
     :refused    the KNOWN posting rows exceed the budget — declined before the
                 read, `:hits []`, `:required-posting-rows` names the cost;
     :incomplete the substrate could not count ahead of the read and the read
                 exceeded the budget — `:hits []`, `:resume` is a provenance
                 cursor for a retry at a higher budget.

   Never returns a partial ranking as `:exact`. A non-positive k is `:exact`
   with no hits."
  [db principal terms k opts]
  (let [budget (get opts :max-posting-rows)]
    (cond
      (<= k 0)
      {:status :exact :hits [] :total nil :candidates 0 :posting-rows 0
       :ranked 0 :examined 0 :budget budget :note :non-positive-k}

      (nil? budget)
      (verdict-from-scores db principal terms k nil (candidate-scores-counted db terms))

      :else
      (let [known (known-posting-rows db terms (inc budget))]
        (if (and (not (nil? known)) (> (get known :rows) budget))
          {:status :refused :reason :posting-budget-exhausted
           :hits [] :total nil :candidates nil
           :posting-rows 0 :ranked 0 :examined 0
           :required-posting-rows (if (get known :capped) nil (get known :rows))
           :required-posting-rows-at-least (get known :rows)
           :budget budget
           :resume (ranked-cursor db principal terms nil)}
          (verdict-from-scores db principal terms k budget (candidate-scores-counted db terms)))))))
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; 6. THE ORDERED SINGLE-TERM POSTING PAGE (genuinely bounded)
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; `:post/rank` encodes (term, score DESC, doc-eid ASC) so that a lexicographic
; AVET prefix read on the term yields the postings ALREADY in rank order, and
; `datom.store/page-datoms` reads that prefix in bounded pages. This is the one
; path in this file whose first page reads O(page + denied) POSTING rows — and
; only on a substrate that implements `datom.store/PageScan`. On ETS the
; documented `scan-via-range-page` fallback materializes the whole prefix, so
; `:bounded?` is false there and callers can tell.

(def ^:private rank-sep "~|~")
(def ^:private rank-field 13)
(def ^:private ordered-page-size 64)

(defn- fixed
  "`x` left-zero-padded to `n` characters, so lexicographic order is numeric
   order. A loop, not `format`/`repeat`, to stay within the kernel's affordances."
  [n x]
  (let [s (str x)]
    (loop [i (- n (count s)) out ""]
      (if (<= i 0) (str out s) (recur (- i 1) (str out "0"))))))

(defn posting-rank-value
  "The `:post/rank` value for a posting: `term` + separator + inverted score
   (fixed width, so DESCENDING) + doc eid (fixed width, ascending). Within one
   term prefix, lexicographic order is exactly (score desc, doc-eid asc)."
  [term tf eid]
  (when (or (not (erlang/is_binary term))
            (not (erlang/is_integer tf)) (< tf 1) (> tf 1000000000000)
            (not (erlang/is_integer eid)) (< eid 0) (>= eid 10000000000000))
    (throw (ex-info "rank value outside the declared fixed-width domain" {:term term :tf tf :eid eid})))
  (str (erlang/byte_size term) ":" term rank-sep
       (fixed rank-field (- 1000000000000 tf)) (fixed rank-field eid)))

(defn- rank-prefix [term] (str (erlang/byte_size term) ":" term rank-sep))

(defn rank-index-tx
  "Transaction data writing `:post/rank` for every posting, from the REAL doc
   eids. This is the derived-projection pass a production pipeline would run
   through the existing derived/reconcile machinery: it needs the postings to
   exist first (so eids are known), which is why it is a second pass rather than
   part of `index-doc-tx`."
  [db]
  (mapv
    (fn [p]
      (let [e (datom/entity db p)]
        {:db/id p
         :post/rank (posting-rank-value (:post/term e) (:post/tf e) (:post/doc e))}))
    (all-posting-eids db)))

(defn- string-prefix-key
  "A store-key prefix matching every AVET entry whose STRING value starts with
   `s`. `datom.index/key-prefix` encodes the string with a trailing 0x00
   terminator, and that terminator is not a byte-prefix of a longer value — so
   it is dropped, leaving `index-tag + attr + TAG-STR + escaped(s)`, which IS a
   proper prefix. (String encoding is order-preserving raw UTF-8 with 0x00
   escaped as 0x00 0xFF, so the prefix is exact even when `s` contains NUL.)"
  [index attr s]
  (let [full (datom.index/key-prefix index attr s)]
    (erlang/binary_part full 0 (- (erlang/byte_size full) 1))))

(defn page-capable?
  "Whether this db value's substrate implements the bounded `PageScan` read.
   Asked of the CAPABILITY target, so a transparent profiler does not answer for
   a substrate that lacks it. False on the ETS development substrate."
  [db]
  (satisfies? datom.store/PageScan (datom.store/capability-target (datom.db/store-of db))))

(defn posting-page
  "One bounded page of `term`'s postings IN RANK ORDER, read from the
   `:post/rank` AVET prefix. Returns
   `{:datoms […] :next key :more? bool :rows n :visible v :bounded? bool}`:
     :page-rows  rows the page RETURNED;
     :rows       rows the store actually READ — the page size when the
                 substrate pages, the whole term's posting count when it fell
                 back, so a budget cannot be fooled by a wide fallback;
     :visible    posting datoms visible in this db value's basis;
     :bounded? whether the read used the substrate's `PageScan` (false ⇒ the
               documented materialize-the-range fallback).
   `after` is a raw store key (exclusive) or nil to start at the term prefix."
  [db term after limit]
  (let [store (datom.db/store-of db)
        lo (string-prefix-key :avet :post/rank (rank-prefix term))
        hi (datom.index/prefix-end lo)
        start (if (nil? after) lo nil)
        res (datom.store/page-datoms store start after hi limit)
        raw (get res :datoms)
        ;; A physical page can split an assertion from its later retraction.
        ;; filter-datoms requires a complete candidate scope for pairing; resolve
        ;; visibility through each posting's complete EAVT attribute instead.
        visible (filterv
                  (fn [d]
                    (and (datom/datom-added? d)
                         (some (fn [current] (= current d))
                               (datom/datoms db :eavt (datom/datom-e d) :post/rank))))
                  raw)]
    {:datoms (vec visible)
     :next (get res :last)
     :more? (not (get res :end?))
     :page-rows (count raw)
     :rows (if (page-capable? db)
             (count raw)
             ; the fallback materialized the whole prefix; report that, not the
             ; page it returned, so the budget counter cannot be fooled
             (get (datom.store/count-range store lo hi 1073741824) :count))
     :visible (count visible)
     :bounded? (page-capable? db)}))

(defn ordered-cursor
  "Provenance cursor for `ordered-topk-page`: a raw `:post/rank` key (exclusive)
   bound to store/basis/term/principal/order."
  [db principal term after]
  {:cursor/kind :ordered-posting
   :cursor/store (store-identity db)
   :cursor/basis (datom/basis-t db)
   :cursor/term term
   :cursor/principal principal
   :cursor/order order-identity-post-rank
   :cursor/after after})

(defn ordered-cursor-ok?
  [db principal term c]
  (and (map? c)
       (not (nil? (get c :cursor/kind)))
       (= (get c :cursor/kind) :ordered-posting)
       (= (:cursor/store c) (store-identity db))
       (= (:cursor/basis c) (datom/basis-t db))
       (= (:cursor/term c) term)
       (= (:cursor/principal c) principal)
       (= (:cursor/order c) order-identity-post-rank)))

(defn- ordered-admission
  "A finite budget cannot admit an unbounded range fallback without a bounded
   Count proving the complete physical rank prefix fits. Unknown refuses."
  [db term budget bounded?]
  (when (and (some? budget) (not bounded?))
    (let [store (datom.db/store-of db)]
      (if (not (datom.store/count-capable? store))
        {:status :refused :reason :unbounded-substrate :hits [] :more? true
         :posting-rows 0 :examined 0 :permitted-seen 0 :bounded? false}
        (let [lo (string-prefix-key :avet :post/rank (rank-prefix term))
              hi (datom.index/prefix-end lo)
              need (datom.store/count-range store lo hi (inc budget))]
          (when (> (:count need) budget)
            {:status :refused :reason :posting-budget-exhausted :hits [] :more? true
             :posting-rows 0 :examined 0 :permitted-seen 0 :bounded? false
             :required-posting-rows-at-least (:count need)}))))))

(defn ordered-topk-page
  "ONE genuinely bounded page of `term`'s authorized top-k, walked in `:post/rank`
   order. `opts` may hold `:cursor` (an `ordered-cursor`) and `:max-posting-rows`.

   Reads posting rows in pages no larger than the remaining budget, applies the
   authorization gate as it goes, and stops at k permitted rows. Verdicts:
     :exact      hits are the true top-k for this term (budget not hit);
     :incomplete the budget ran out before k was found — `:hits []` and a
                 `:resume` cursor, never a partial ranking;
     :refused    the cursor's provenance does not match this db/term/principal.
   `:bounded?` is false when the substrate fell back to materializing the range,
   so the caller can tell a genuinely bounded page from a correct-but-wide one.
   A non-positive k is `:exact` with no hits."
  [db principal term k opts]
  (let [budget (get opts :max-posting-rows)
        cursor (get opts :cursor)
        bounded? (page-capable? db)]
    (cond
      (<= k 0)
      {:status :exact :hits [] :cursor nil :more? false :posting-rows 0
       :examined 0 :permitted-seen 0 :bounded? bounded? :note :non-positive-k}

      (and (not (nil? cursor)) (not (ordered-cursor-ok? db principal term cursor)))
      {:status :refused :reason :cursor-mismatch :hits [] :cursor nil :more? false
       :posting-rows 0 :examined 0 :permitted-seen 0 :bounded? bounded?}

      (or (not (erlang/is_binary term))
          (and (some? budget) (or (not (erlang/is_integer budget)) (< budget 0))))
      {:status :refused :reason :invalid-query-budget :hits [] :cursor nil
       :posting-rows 0 :examined 0 :bounded? bounded?}

      :else
      (let [admission (ordered-admission db term budget bounded?)]
        (if admission
          (assoc admission :resume (ordered-cursor db principal term (get cursor :cursor/after)))
          (loop [after (get cursor :cursor/after nil) kept [] rows 0 examined 0]
        (if (and (not (nil? budget)) (>= rows budget))
          (if (= k (count kept))
            {:status :exact :hits (vec kept) :cursor nil :more? false
             :posting-rows rows :examined examined :permitted-seen (count kept) :bounded? bounded?}
            {:status :incomplete :reason :posting-budget-exhausted
             :hits [] :resume (ordered-cursor db principal term (get cursor :cursor/after)) :more? true
             :posting-rows rows :examined examined :permitted-seen (count kept) :bounded? bounded?})
          ;; Never fetch more visible candidates than the remaining hit slots.
          ;; Otherwise returning the store's end-of-page cursor would skip
          ;; unexamined candidates from the same physical page.
          (let [limit (max 1 (min ordered-page-size (- k (count kept))
                                   (if (nil? budget) ordered-page-size (- budget rows))))
                pg (posting-page db term after limit)
                ds (get pg :datoms)
                raw (get pg :rows)
                step (reduce
                       (fn [acc d]
                         (if (>= (count (get acc :kept)) k)
                           acc
                           (let [p (datom/datom-e d)
                                 e (datom/entity db p)
                                 doc (:post/doc e)
                                 score (:post/tf e)]
                             (if (authorized? db principal doc)
                               {:kept (conj (get acc :kept) {:eid doc :score score})
                                :examined (+ (get acc :examined) 1)}
                               {:kept (get acc :kept)
                                :examined (+ (get acc :examined) 1)}))))
                       {:kept kept :examined examined}
                       ds)
                kept2 (get step :kept)
                examined2 (get step :examined)
                rows2 (+ rows raw)]
            (cond
              (and (not (nil? budget)) (> rows2 budget))
              {:status :incomplete :reason :substrate-budget-exceeded
               :hits [] :resume (ordered-cursor db principal term (get cursor :cursor/after))
               :more? true :posting-rows rows2 :examined examined2
               :permitted-seen (count kept2) :bounded? (get pg :bounded?)}

              (>= (count kept2) k)
              {:status :exact :hits (vec kept2)
               :cursor (ordered-cursor db principal term (get pg :next))
               :more? (get pg :more?)
               :posting-rows rows2 :examined examined2
               :permitted-seen (count kept2) :bounded? (get pg :bounded?)}

              (not (get pg :more?))
              {:status :exact :hits (vec kept2) :cursor nil :more? false
               :posting-rows rows2 :examined examined2
               :permitted-seen (count kept2) :bounded? (get pg :bounded?)}

              :else
              (recur (get pg :next) kept2 rows2 examined2))))))))))

; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; 7. INDEX MAINTENANCE — a small prototype of the posting lifecycle
; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; The candidate path is only as good as the postings. These two primitives are
; the maintenance seam a real pipeline would drive from the derived/projection
; machinery: one transaction to add a document and its postings, one to retire a
; document and its postings together (so a tombstone cannot leave a dangling
; posting that still matches). `:post/rank` is a SECOND, derived pass
; (`rank-index-tx`) because it keys on the real doc eid.

(defn posting-tx
  "The posting rows for document record `d`
   ({:id :text :tf …}): one posting per whitespace-separated term. Posting ids
   are derived from the document's tempid, so they are unique within a fixture
   and never collide with document or principal tempids."
  [d]
  (when (or (not (erlang/is_integer (:tf d))) (< (:tf d) 1) (> (:tf d) 1000000000000))
    (throw (ex-info "posting frequency outside the declared positive domain" {:tf (:tf d)})))
  (map-indexed
    (fn [i w]
      {:db/id (- (* (:id d) 100000) i)
       :post/term w
       :post/doc (:id d)
       :post/tf (:tf d)})
    (String/split (:text d) " ")))

(defn index-doc-tx
  "Transaction data adding one document and its postings. `:live` is always
   written (so the gate has an explicit value); `:public`/`:readers` only when
   supplied. Does NOT write `:post/rank`; run `rank-index-tx` after the docs
   exist."
  [d]
  (into [{:db/id (:id d) :doc/text (:text d) :doc/live (:live d)}]
        (concat
          (if (:public d) [{:db/id (:id d) :doc/public true}] [])
          (if (seq (:readers d)) [{:db/id (:id d) :doc/reader (:readers d)}] [])
          (posting-tx d))))

(defn retract-doc!
  "Retire a document AND its postings atomically: reads the document's postings
   through the AVET index on `:post/doc`, then retracts every posting and the
   document in one transaction. After this the candidate path can neither match
   the document by a stale posting nor authorize it."
  [conn doc-id]
  (let [db (datom/db conn)
        ps (mapv datom/datom-e (datom/datoms db :avet :post/doc doc-id))]
    (datom/transact! conn
                     (into (mapv (fn [p] [:db/retractEntity p]) ps)
                           [[:db/retractEntity doc-id]]))))
